---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="10-decomposition"
  lessonSlug="00-example-distance-incremental"
  hook="Build distance() in four steps. Each step runs. Each step verifies one new thing. The textbook's most-quoted example of incremental development."
>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`import math

def area(radius):
    return math.pi * radius ** 2

def distance(x1, y1, x2, y2):
    dx = x2 - x1
    dy = y2 - y1
    return math.sqrt(dx ** 2 + dy ** 2)

def circle_area(xc, yc, xp, yp):
    radius = distance(xc, yc, xp, yp)
    return area(radius)

print("distance:", distance(1, 2, 4, 6))     # 5.0  (3-4-5 triangle)
print("area(5):", area(5))                   # ~78.54
print("circle_area:", circle_area(0, 0, 3, 4)) # area of radius 5 circle`}
  />

  <Callout tone="note" title="Source: Think Python §6.2">
    <p>
      <em>"The goal of incremental development is to avoid long debugging
      sessions by adding and testing only a small amount of code at a time."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The four-step build</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Skeleton</strong>: <code>return 0.0</code> — verify it runs</li>
    <li><strong>Compute dx, dy</strong>: print them — verify diff calculation</li>
    <li><strong>Sum of squares</strong>: print dsquared — verify it equals 25</li>
    <li><strong>sqrt + remove scaffolding</strong>: final form</li>
  </ol>

  <Callout tone="tip" title="Composition (§6.3)">
    <p>
      <code>circle_area</code> is THREE lines because the hard parts already
      exist as <code>distance</code> and <code>area</code>. If
      <code>circle_area</code> is wrong, you have 3 lines to check — and
      you've already tested the other two functions.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
